WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.326 --> 00:00:02.476
- Hi everyone Sal, here from Khan Academy,

00:00:02.476 --> 00:00:04.436
welcome to our Homeroom live stream,

00:00:04.436 --> 00:00:06.166
very excited about the conversation

00:00:06.166 --> 00:00:08.226
we're about to have with Lester Holt.

00:00:08.226 --> 00:00:09.856
Before we jump into that conversation,

00:00:09.856 --> 00:00:13.346
I will make a few of my
standard announcements one,

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.726
just a reminder to folks that Khan Academy

00:00:15.726 --> 00:00:17.996
is an out-for-profit if
you're in a position to do so,

00:00:17.996 --> 00:00:20.466
please think about making
a donation that can be

00:00:20.466 --> 00:00:23.676
done at khan academy.org/donate.

00:00:23.676 --> 00:00:26.506
Also wanna give special thanks
to several organizations

00:00:26.506 --> 00:00:28.856
that have stepped up over
the last couple of months

00:00:28.856 --> 00:00:30.676
when they saw that we were
already running into deficit

00:00:30.676 --> 00:00:33.966
pre COVID and that the
deficit only grew as we saw

00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:37.056
300% of the traffic we normally
see as all of the folks

00:00:37.056 --> 00:00:39.786
are leaning on us for as we figure out

00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:41.276
distance learning together.

00:00:41.276 --> 00:00:43.496
Special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:00:43.496 --> 00:00:46.796
AT&amp;T and Fastly, Novartis and
the Amgen family foundation,

00:00:46.796 --> 00:00:49.026
Amgen Foundation, not
the family foundation.

00:00:49.026 --> 00:00:52.166
I also wanna announce, I
announced this on a previous

00:00:52.166 --> 00:00:55.505
live stream that we're
taking select versions

00:00:55.505 --> 00:00:58.566
of this live stream and
turning them into a podcast.

00:00:58.566 --> 00:01:01.786
So if you want to watch, or
maybe you shouldn't be watching,

00:01:01.786 --> 00:01:03.936
if you wanna listen to this,
maybe while you're in the car

00:01:03.936 --> 00:01:06.516
or some other place we're taking a subset

00:01:06.516 --> 00:01:09.146
of our conversations, editing
them a little bit for podcast

00:01:09.146 --> 00:01:11.946
and making them available
so you can anywhere you,

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:14.486
you can get your podcast
hopefully you'll see

00:01:14.486 --> 00:01:16.976
the Homeroom with Sal shortly.

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:20.866
So with that, I'm really
excited and honored to introduce

00:01:20.866 --> 00:01:23.186
someone that you're probably
already familiar with,

00:01:23.186 --> 00:01:25.466
someone who you've probably
already seen in your home,

00:01:25.466 --> 00:01:28.426
a Lester Holt, anchor
of the NBC Nightly News

00:01:28.426 --> 00:01:32.696
with Lester Holt, Lester,
thanks so much for joining us.

00:01:32.696 --> 00:01:35.736
- Happy to join you Sal and
yes, people watch me at home

00:01:35.736 --> 00:01:38.959
and it's basically from my
home to their home these days.

00:01:40.336 --> 00:01:42.596
- Yeah, I think there's
something nice about that.

00:01:42.596 --> 00:01:44.546
I know it's above my pay grade,

00:01:44.546 --> 00:01:46.086
but you might wanna continue this,

00:01:46.086 --> 00:01:48.306
It's very intimate in a nice way.

00:01:48.306 --> 00:01:52.156
- Well, it's a lot more
responsibility than I'm used to

00:01:52.156 --> 00:01:54.216
normally I go into a big
studio and I don't think

00:01:54.216 --> 00:01:57.076
about cameras and sound and
lighting and all that stuff

00:01:57.076 --> 00:02:01.076
now I do, and now my big
fear is tripping over a plug,

00:02:01.076 --> 00:02:04.786
which has been done and
frightening our signal out of here

00:02:04.786 --> 00:02:09.186
but, it's, I always tell
people that as a international

00:02:09.186 --> 00:02:11.836
loose organization, we're
used to taking the broadcast

00:02:11.836 --> 00:02:14.376
on the road to far-flung
corners of the world

00:02:14.376 --> 00:02:16.916
in hospitable places so in many ways,

00:02:16.916 --> 00:02:20.946
this wasn't a huge challenge
for us but in a sustaining way

00:02:20.946 --> 00:02:23.656
to do our broadcast like this has been

00:02:23.656 --> 00:02:27.046
it's been interesting and I
certainly miss the face to face

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:28.769
collaboration with my colleagues.

00:02:29.726 --> 00:02:31.696
- I could imagine. I mean you're doing it

00:02:31.696 --> 00:02:33.356
at a much higher stakes before I even do

00:02:33.356 --> 00:02:36.706
this live stream, I go
through my house yelling,

00:02:36.706 --> 00:02:38.936
get off of live stream,
get off of Netflix,

00:02:38.936 --> 00:02:41.879
I'm about to get (laughs),
get off of zoom everybody.

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:42.712
(laughs)

00:02:42.712 --> 00:02:44.116
So I can have all the...

00:02:44.116 --> 00:02:48.086
- I have a dog that occasionally
piped in and then last week

00:02:48.086 --> 00:02:52.516
I was watching my grandsons
and the one-year-old,

00:02:52.516 --> 00:02:55.496
I'm on the phone he runs into
my little makeshift studio

00:02:55.496 --> 00:02:58.256
and of all the things he
touches it's the button

00:02:58.256 --> 00:02:59.736
that could knock me off the air.

00:02:59.736 --> 00:03:01.356
And I look on the screen and it says,

00:03:01.356 --> 00:03:02.826
do you really wish to disconnect?

00:03:02.826 --> 00:03:06.066
I'm like, no(laughs)
so it's...(indistinct)

00:03:06.066 --> 00:03:08.166
everyone's going through it on some level.

00:03:09.136 --> 00:03:12.456
- No, it's really interesting
and so, so there's a bunch

00:03:12.456 --> 00:03:14.996
of stuff I'm excited to talk
to you about and I do wanna

00:03:14.996 --> 00:03:18.446
encourage anyone watching
on Facebook or YouTube

00:03:18.446 --> 00:03:20.366
or social media generally every
now and then this shows up

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.036
in other places I didn't expect.

00:03:22.036 --> 00:03:24.186
Put questions and we have
team members who are gonna

00:03:24.186 --> 00:03:27.816
surface the questions
for Lester and myself

00:03:27.816 --> 00:03:30.876
if it's relevant and maybe
a good place to start,

00:03:30.876 --> 00:03:31.926
which I think is really
exciting 'cause we have a lot

00:03:31.926 --> 00:03:33.936
of young people watching
this and a lot of parents

00:03:33.936 --> 00:03:37.256
who watch this is, not
only are you the anchor

00:03:37.256 --> 00:03:39.656
of the NBC Nightly News,
you're also the anchor

00:03:39.656 --> 00:03:42.856
of the NBC Nightly News Kids Edition,

00:03:42.856 --> 00:03:45.716
which is available online
if someone were to search

00:03:45.716 --> 00:03:48.246
that look it up, which I
love I've actually watched

00:03:48.246 --> 00:03:49.966
many many episodes of it.

00:03:49.966 --> 00:03:53.556
Tell us why, why you did
this and what, what this is.

00:03:53.556 --> 00:03:56.616
- Well, this was early on
in the pandemic and things

00:03:56.616 --> 00:03:58.456
were going south very quickly, we were all

00:03:58.456 --> 00:04:01.196
being overwhelmed and I
remember doing the newscast

00:04:01.196 --> 00:04:04.026
every night and thinking
there are families watching

00:04:04.026 --> 00:04:07.636
this right now and the numbers
and the stories we're telling

00:04:07.636 --> 00:04:08.846
are grim and these video diaries

00:04:08.846 --> 00:04:13.546
and these healthcare workers
and one of the other producers

00:04:13.546 --> 00:04:15.017
on the broadcast said,

00:04:15.017 --> 00:04:17.596
"maybe we should do like a
kid show geared toward them."

00:04:17.596 --> 00:04:19.506
And we all looked at
each other like of course

00:04:19.506 --> 00:04:21.873
that's exactly what we should be doing.

00:04:21.873 --> 00:04:24.666
So, it started out very
organic just a handful of folks

00:04:24.666 --> 00:04:27.946
kind of scrapping the other
and trying to learn as we go

00:04:27.946 --> 00:04:31.026
along about a way to present
some of this very heavy stuff

00:04:31.026 --> 00:04:34.876
with this pandemic to
kids in a digestible way.

00:04:34.876 --> 00:04:37.086
I mean kids aren't stupid they're watching

00:04:37.086 --> 00:04:39.246
what's going on around them,
they're seeing the confusion

00:04:39.246 --> 00:04:41.576
and distress among the
adults and we wanted to do

00:04:41.576 --> 00:04:42.606
it in bite sizes.

00:04:42.606 --> 00:04:45.486
So, we've kept that up and
we've tried to also move

00:04:45.486 --> 00:04:49.031
along with the news, so of
course we covered the...

00:04:49.031 --> 00:04:52.396
some of the protests and the
Black Lives Matter Movement

00:04:52.396 --> 00:04:54.896
and things happening in
space and other things

00:04:54.896 --> 00:04:57.516
that might be of interest of
specific interest to kids.

00:04:57.516 --> 00:05:01.096
And we like to feature kids to
some of the inspiring things

00:05:01.096 --> 00:05:04.046
that they're doing, how they've,
figuring out ways to learn

00:05:04.046 --> 00:05:07.899
at home and just kind
of adapt to our reality.

00:05:09.626 --> 00:05:11.846
- No and I'll once again and
say, it's really interesting

00:05:11.846 --> 00:05:14.200
and I've actually been very
impressed, you take questions

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.033
from kids and the questions that they ask

00:05:15.033 --> 00:05:18.220
are oftentimes better
than some of what you see

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.755
in the experts(laughs) asking
on, on the adult on, on,

00:05:20.755 --> 00:05:25.516
on, on television, generally
I think one of the really

00:05:25.516 --> 00:05:28.916
interesting things that a lot
of folks wonder and I used

00:05:28.916 --> 00:05:31.599
to wonder this when I would
see a Lester Holt on TV,

00:05:32.556 --> 00:05:34.226
how did you become Lester Holt?

00:05:34.226 --> 00:05:38.626
How do you, how do you
become kind of this trusted

00:05:38.626 --> 00:05:42.727
go to person for a whole
country or for the world,

00:05:42.727 --> 00:05:44.986
did you think you were going
to do this when you were

00:05:44.986 --> 00:05:47.276
a teenager when you were younger or?

00:05:47.276 --> 00:05:49.826
There's a question here from
Neupro Mantha on YouTube

00:05:49.826 --> 00:05:50.986
what were your struggles you encountered?

00:05:50.986 --> 00:05:52.786
A lot of people think
it's I wanted to do it

00:05:52.786 --> 00:05:54.316
and then it just happened all of a sudden.

00:05:54.316 --> 00:05:56.916
I'd love to hear a little
bit about your journey.

00:05:56.916 --> 00:05:58.526
- Things just unfolded for me,

00:05:58.526 --> 00:06:01.196
I did wanna be in broadcasting
from a very early age

00:06:01.196 --> 00:06:03.406
I had an older brother
eight years older than me

00:06:03.406 --> 00:06:07.196
who had a brief stint in
radio and he took me down

00:06:07.196 --> 00:06:08.666
to the radio station one day and I thought

00:06:08.666 --> 00:06:12.206
this is very very cool and so
that's what I really wanted

00:06:12.206 --> 00:06:16.756
to do was radio and be
a DJ at the age of 17

00:06:16.756 --> 00:06:18.156
during my senior year in high school,

00:06:18.156 --> 00:06:21.886
I got a job at a country
in Western radio station

00:06:21.886 --> 00:06:25.816
in Sacramento, California,
midnight to six on Saturdays

00:06:25.816 --> 00:06:30.816
and Sundays playing records and
reading the news every hour.

00:06:31.196 --> 00:06:34.466
At the same time I was got an internship,

00:06:34.466 --> 00:06:36.146
wasn't even a real
internship 'cause I was still

00:06:36.146 --> 00:06:38.186
in high school but I managed
to kind of talk my way

00:06:38.186 --> 00:06:40.956
in to the NBC station in Sacramento,

00:06:40.956 --> 00:06:44.226
the TV station, KCR, right
and kind of hung around there

00:06:44.226 --> 00:06:46.306
and helped out in the
public affairs department

00:06:46.306 --> 00:06:49.286
and just tried to soak
up as much as I could.

00:06:49.286 --> 00:06:51.566
Started college, did
two years in the college

00:06:51.566 --> 00:06:53.936
and landed a job in San Francisco

00:06:53.936 --> 00:06:57.056
at an all news radio
station so I dropped out,

00:06:57.056 --> 00:07:00.096
never went back, kids don't do what I do,

00:07:00.096 --> 00:07:04.579
don't do what I do but life
kind of worked out did radio,

00:07:05.686 --> 00:07:10.456
went to New York when I was
22, maybe no yeah about 22

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:16.186
and landed a job at the CVS
TV station here then stayed

00:07:16.186 --> 00:07:19.246
with CVS, went to Los
Angeles and back to New York

00:07:19.246 --> 00:07:23.626
then Chicago, all in local
news and then came to MSNBC

00:07:24.641 --> 00:07:28.256
in 2000, and it was the
beginning of this incredible

00:07:28.256 --> 00:07:30.556
five year period of news.

00:07:30.556 --> 00:07:33.466
I got there, it's just
kind of back to the future

00:07:33.466 --> 00:07:36.436
but during the Biden I'm sorry

00:07:37.555 --> 00:07:39.746
during the Gore Bush
presidential race and of course

00:07:39.746 --> 00:07:43.156
the recount in Florida,
and from that before long

00:07:43.156 --> 00:07:47.216
we were staring at 911, the
invasion of Afghanistan,

00:07:47.216 --> 00:07:49.446
the invasion of Iraq, that
was all in that really

00:07:49.446 --> 00:07:52.486
brief period of time, and
I was principal anchor

00:07:52.486 --> 00:07:54.316
for a lot of that and
then kind of worked my way

00:07:54.316 --> 00:07:59.316
across the river to NBC
news and weekend today,

00:07:59.636 --> 00:08:04.526
weekend Nightly News, Dateline
and finally five years ago

00:08:04.526 --> 00:08:05.949
landed in this job.

00:08:07.416 --> 00:08:09.246
- And then what would you
say I mean that's actually

00:08:09.246 --> 00:08:11.976
an incredible journey I the
fact that you were doing

00:08:11.976 --> 00:08:13.871
so much even from such a young age

00:08:13.871 --> 00:08:16.696
and you kind of knew
this, I am curious what

00:08:16.696 --> 00:08:18.616
and this is sometimes
hard for people to answer

00:08:18.616 --> 00:08:21.906
'cause they don't wanna seem
they wanna be modest about it,

00:08:21.906 --> 00:08:23.596
but please don't be modest.

00:08:23.596 --> 00:08:27.696
What would you think allowed
you to kind of stand out

00:08:27.696 --> 00:08:31.046
and eventually, essentially
have the top job

00:08:31.046 --> 00:08:33.986
in broadcasting, at least
from my vantage point?

00:08:33.986 --> 00:08:38.666
- I played the long game and I
just took it a step at a time

00:08:38.666 --> 00:08:40.956
and really never believed
in it could be like

00:08:40.956 --> 00:08:42.336
this like overnight success

00:08:42.336 --> 00:08:43.746
with that kind of paying your dues.

00:08:43.746 --> 00:08:45.786
And a lot of people I worked with talked

00:08:45.786 --> 00:08:47.476
about the importance of paying your dues

00:08:47.476 --> 00:08:50.119
and learning your craft
and just listening.

00:08:51.146 --> 00:08:54.346
I was blessed with good
mentors, people that gave me

00:08:54.346 --> 00:08:57.786
sage advice and who admonish
me when I got something wrong

00:08:57.786 --> 00:09:01.246
or the script didn't make
any sense or whatever it was

00:09:01.246 --> 00:09:04.586
and I just tried to give
everything in each job

00:09:04.586 --> 00:09:06.716
and then opportunities
would present themselves.

00:09:06.716 --> 00:09:08.479
One guy workforce said,

00:09:09.513 --> 00:09:12.286
"you've gotta be ready when
doors open because doors open

00:09:12.286 --> 00:09:15.705
at times you don't expect
circumstances you don't expect

00:09:15.705 --> 00:09:18.086
but you gotta be ready
to walk through them."

00:09:18.086 --> 00:09:21.256
And that's kind of what my
philosophy has always been,

00:09:21.256 --> 00:09:23.966
I've always said to people
before I got this job,

00:09:23.966 --> 00:09:27.838
I was anchoring three jobs
at NBC news and I said,

00:09:27.838 --> 00:09:32.526
"sometimes we're so busy
climbing the ladder that we fail

00:09:32.526 --> 00:09:35.196
to like stop and pause on a
wrong and kind of look out

00:09:35.196 --> 00:09:36.626
and enjoy the view."

00:09:36.626 --> 00:09:40.286
And, I was in place that
wasn't necessarily the top

00:09:40.286 --> 00:09:42.226
of the game but for me
I was really enjoying

00:09:42.226 --> 00:09:45.606
what I was doing and so when
the opportunity came to anchor

00:09:45.606 --> 00:09:48.809
at Nightly News, I was prepared
to walk through that door.

00:09:50.076 --> 00:09:52.926
But at the same time I
was perfectly happy doing

00:09:52.926 --> 00:09:54.006
what I was doing before.

00:09:54.006 --> 00:09:59.006
I love covering news, I love
taking my personal curiosity

00:09:59.056 --> 00:10:03.666
and making that my profession
and I just every move

00:10:03.666 --> 00:10:05.276
has been I've been tickled by it,

00:10:05.276 --> 00:10:07.336
it's been hey, this is great,
it's a new opportunity,

00:10:07.336 --> 00:10:08.256
and a new chance.

00:10:08.256 --> 00:10:11.246
But I try to think of that
if that's the last job I had,

00:10:11.246 --> 00:10:12.199
it's a great job.

00:10:13.796 --> 00:10:16.126
- And what do you think when
you look at other people?

00:10:16.126 --> 00:10:18.246
Young people especially who
are coming through the ranks

00:10:18.246 --> 00:10:23.246
maybe 20 or 30 years before an
earlier version of yourself.

00:10:23.686 --> 00:10:25.720
Where do you do you sometimes
look at people like okay

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.246
that young one is gonna make
something out of themselves

00:10:29.246 --> 00:10:31.766
because of X, Y, and Z and that
person's probably not doing

00:10:31.766 --> 00:10:33.026
it the right way.

00:10:33.026 --> 00:10:35.656
- Yeah I do see there's I
mean when I whenever I meet

00:10:35.656 --> 00:10:38.056
someone who says I'm gonna
be an anchor I'm like

00:10:38.936 --> 00:10:40.956
what you wanna be is
you wanna be a reporter

00:10:40.956 --> 00:10:43.283
another mentor I had said,

00:10:43.283 --> 00:10:46.586
"being a reporter is the
highest calling of what we do."

00:10:46.586 --> 00:10:49.176
And I say that to say
this, that when I'm sitting

00:10:49.176 --> 00:10:53.426
on that set every night,
yes it's prestigious,

00:10:53.426 --> 00:10:56.016
it's the top of the
game, it pays more money

00:10:56.016 --> 00:10:59.376
and a lot of good things about
it but the most rewarding

00:10:59.376 --> 00:11:01.786
and interesting and fascinating
moments of my career

00:11:01.786 --> 00:11:03.916
have not been sitting at an anchor desk.

00:11:03.916 --> 00:11:06.296
They've been out on the
field, with camera crews

00:11:06.296 --> 00:11:09.586
and a pen and pad and fighting deadlines

00:11:09.586 --> 00:11:12.616
and whatever hurricanes or toxic leaks

00:11:12.616 --> 00:11:13.912
whatever the story is.

00:11:13.912 --> 00:11:16.236
It was just being there
and covering the story

00:11:16.236 --> 00:11:17.976
that's what we do.

00:11:17.976 --> 00:11:21.236
I am always gonna be a
reporter first as I said,

00:11:21.236 --> 00:11:23.916
being an anchor, it's the big
deal, it's a celebrated job,

00:11:23.916 --> 00:11:26.996
very cool to get my name on
the wall and all that stuff

00:11:26.996 --> 00:11:29.796
but it's all about, it's all
about the art of reporting.

00:11:30.746 --> 00:11:33.016
- Do you still get to do
some of that reporting

00:11:33.016 --> 00:11:35.746
is that part of your deal that
you'll be able to maybe do

00:11:35.746 --> 00:11:39.726
some travel or do some
investigative type reporting?

00:11:39.726 --> 00:11:43.106
- I do, I am one of the hallmarks
of this show is we take it

00:11:43.106 --> 00:11:47.076
on the road a lot for big
stories, be it a natural disaster

00:11:47.076 --> 00:11:51.116
or terrorists or attacks
or big big stories.

00:11:51.116 --> 00:11:54.356
It's been difficult for us to
do that during the pandemic,

00:11:54.356 --> 00:11:58.016
traveling and and
quarantines and things of...

00:11:58.016 --> 00:11:59.136
make things different.

00:11:59.136 --> 00:12:03.966
I did travel though to Minneapolis during

00:12:03.966 --> 00:12:07.886
the George Floyd story,
we then traveled to Texas

00:12:07.886 --> 00:12:09.406
for the funeral there.

00:12:09.406 --> 00:12:12.806
So we've gotten out some
but we're still making

00:12:12.806 --> 00:12:15.336
the adjustments but yeah
I still like to get out

00:12:15.336 --> 00:12:18.236
and tell stories and I still
work for Dateline, I'm sorry

00:12:18.236 --> 00:12:22.706
I just hit the camera, I
still do Dateline projects,

00:12:22.706 --> 00:12:24.776
I'm working on one right now.

00:12:24.776 --> 00:12:27.106
So, that's what I love
to do and like all of us

00:12:27.106 --> 00:12:29.456
we're kind of figuring
out what's possible,

00:12:29.456 --> 00:12:34.446
how do we adjust, how
we work in this pandemic

00:12:34.446 --> 00:12:36.046
but we're we're figuring it out.

00:12:37.536 --> 00:12:40.016
- And there's an interesting
question it's kind of related

00:12:40.016 --> 00:12:43.866
to that from Facebook Ellen H Omen.

00:12:43.866 --> 00:12:47.176
And, I guess the context
for this is, the news

00:12:47.176 --> 00:12:50.936
when you watch the news, you
learn about if God forbid

00:12:50.936 --> 00:12:54.796
a plane goes down or something
bad happens or death counts

00:12:54.796 --> 00:12:57.106
from the pandemic that
tends to be oftentimes

00:12:57.106 --> 00:12:58.279
like the leading news.

00:12:59.296 --> 00:13:00.966
But the question from Ellen is,

00:13:00.966 --> 00:13:02.776
can we have some good news shows too,

00:13:02.776 --> 00:13:04.726
wouldn't that be amazing?

00:13:04.726 --> 00:13:07.636
Is there a kind of a movement
maybe to try to surface

00:13:07.636 --> 00:13:09.936
you're not just the seven
really bad things that happened

00:13:09.936 --> 00:13:12.296
in the world but maybe
get people in a good mood

00:13:12.296 --> 00:13:14.726
and I say that actually my
mom, I think is addicted

00:13:14.726 --> 00:13:18.449
to the news and sometimes it
gets to (laughs) her I think.

00:13:18.449 --> 00:13:19.846
- No alright listen, I
tell people all the time

00:13:19.846 --> 00:13:22.796
they look at me really I
say there are days that I do

00:13:22.796 --> 00:13:25.876
the newscasts and I walk out of the studio

00:13:25.876 --> 00:13:29.826
wow that was tough, like
nobody lived in that broadcast

00:13:29.826 --> 00:13:32.526
there we can't obviously
dictate I mean some days

00:13:32.526 --> 00:13:35.996
are just super heavy but
what we can do is try

00:13:36.962 --> 00:13:38.966
and have an arc of
stories in the broadcast

00:13:38.966 --> 00:13:41.006
and that's what we aim to do so yes,

00:13:41.006 --> 00:13:44.886
there will be whatever the
top story is, there may be

00:13:44.886 --> 00:13:47.436
then stories are dealing with
particular issues going on,

00:13:47.436 --> 00:13:50.586
but we always like to end
the broadcast on something

00:13:50.586 --> 00:13:53.786
uplifting some story we
have different franchises,

00:13:53.786 --> 00:13:57.296
inspiring America is one of
them and stories where people

00:13:57.296 --> 00:14:00.186
are going above and beyond,
they are taking on an issue

00:14:00.186 --> 00:14:03.546
by themselves, they are reaching
out to their fellow man.

00:14:03.546 --> 00:14:07.743
And we, I've had people stop
me and ask about that thing,

00:14:07.743 --> 00:14:09.286
"I love those stories at the end."

00:14:09.286 --> 00:14:12.116
And sometimes I see you tears in your eyes

00:14:12.116 --> 00:14:15.006
and yeah that happens,
I'm a big crier sometimes,

00:14:15.006 --> 00:14:18.636
but I think it's really
important that we find ways

00:14:18.636 --> 00:14:20.776
as often as we can again
we can't always dictate

00:14:20.776 --> 00:14:22.736
what the news day will bring but find ways

00:14:22.736 --> 00:14:26.786
to kinda center people on
the good that we all share

00:14:26.786 --> 00:14:30.676
that we hear so much about the
divisiveness in this country

00:14:30.676 --> 00:14:34.936
and it is real but there's
also just a lot of people

00:14:34.936 --> 00:14:37.306
who aren't asking what
your political stripes are

00:14:37.306 --> 00:14:39.436
and they just wanna help,
and they just wanna make

00:14:39.436 --> 00:14:41.796
a difference in this world
and it's part of our job

00:14:41.796 --> 00:14:42.739
to recognize them.

00:14:43.786 --> 00:14:46.768
- Yeah no, it makes a lot
of sense that I mean related

00:14:46.768 --> 00:14:48.880
to that I mean this is a
really interesting question

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:49.816
from Facebook Fay Marie,

00:14:49.816 --> 00:14:51.926
how do you keep up with what's important

00:14:51.926 --> 00:14:55.936
yet not get overwhelmed by
the volume of news out there?

00:14:55.936 --> 00:14:59.476
- Okay, I'm just gonna tell
is it Fay, is that the person?

00:14:59.476 --> 00:15:00.486
- Fay.

00:15:00.486 --> 00:15:03.759
- Okay Fay, sometimes I don't watch.

00:15:03.759 --> 00:15:06.602
And(giggles) I say, I
couldn't say that but then I

00:15:06.602 --> 00:15:09.326
during the when we were first
going through the beginning

00:15:09.326 --> 00:15:12.676
of this pandemic, I would have
friends say things like that.

00:15:12.676 --> 00:15:15.636
That I can't handle it, and
you start to worry about people

00:15:15.636 --> 00:15:18.396
seriously about people's
ability to deal with this,

00:15:18.396 --> 00:15:22.726
and I would say sometimes you
just gotta walk away from it.

00:15:22.726 --> 00:15:26.686
You've gotta turn off the TV
and that's the last message

00:15:26.686 --> 00:15:28.026
I know you would wanna
hear from me and I hope

00:15:28.026 --> 00:15:30.316
you'll watch me and
then turn it off later.

00:15:30.316 --> 00:15:32.406
But what I mean by I don't
mean to be flippant but I think

00:15:32.406 --> 00:15:35.756
you do have to give yourself
a breather and sometimes

00:15:35.756 --> 00:15:38.236
take it in small doses.

00:15:38.236 --> 00:15:41.166
We've been through some
really difficult times

00:15:41.166 --> 00:15:45.826
we've been put in the pandemic,
the protests in the streets,

00:15:45.826 --> 00:15:48.706
struggling the Black
Lives Matter Movement,

00:15:48.706 --> 00:15:50.866
these are all important things
that we're struggling with

00:15:50.866 --> 00:15:51.966
in different ways.

00:15:51.966 --> 00:15:55.376
You've got the politics
firing at us right now,

00:15:55.376 --> 00:16:00.376
you need to be informed,
you can't totally unplug

00:16:00.996 --> 00:16:03.826
but take breaks, give
yourself a moment and try

00:16:03.826 --> 00:16:05.186
and digest these things.

00:16:05.186 --> 00:16:08.566
It's helpful to me, believe
it or not there's sometimes

00:16:08.566 --> 00:16:10.246
during the day when I'm working, of course

00:16:10.246 --> 00:16:12.506
I have to be plugged in,
but there are some weekends

00:16:12.506 --> 00:16:14.586
where I have to say you know
I'm gonna take the morning off.

00:16:14.586 --> 00:16:18.316
Now, I have my phone, if something
big happens, I can watch,

00:16:18.316 --> 00:16:20.566
I get this text alerts
still, I know what's going on

00:16:20.566 --> 00:16:23.686
in the news, but I do
give myself breathers

00:16:23.686 --> 00:16:25.286
and I think it could be healthy.

00:16:26.406 --> 00:16:28.236
- Yeah. I couldn't agree
more and I think if anything

00:16:28.236 --> 00:16:29.996
it can give you more perspective
if you have a breather

00:16:29.996 --> 00:16:32.586
you can digest it and think
about it more as a historian

00:16:32.586 --> 00:16:35.106
than someone just caught up in the moment.

00:16:35.106 --> 00:16:37.426
So, it makes a ton of sense.

00:16:37.426 --> 00:16:39.656
There's another question I
think from people hearing your,

00:16:39.656 --> 00:16:42.556
I think pretty impressive
narrative especially in how much

00:16:42.556 --> 00:16:45.136
motivation and how common
agents you exercised

00:16:45.136 --> 00:16:47.086
when you were young this is from YouTube

00:16:47.086 --> 00:16:50.166
Wakrina M says, you found
your passion pretty early,

00:16:50.166 --> 00:16:53.166
what advice do you give for
kids regarding passionate

00:16:53.166 --> 00:16:56.039
careers, what should they
look for, how do they know?

00:16:56.876 --> 00:16:59.336
- I think I had several passions.

00:16:59.336 --> 00:17:01.936
Music is one of my other
passions, am a bass player,

00:17:01.936 --> 00:17:04.576
and I used to play in
a band I say used to,

00:17:04.576 --> 00:17:09.576
we're not able to get together
anymore, but I was blessed

00:17:09.676 --> 00:17:13.876
to be raised by two wonderful
parents who basically

00:17:13.876 --> 00:17:16.156
instilled this idea
that we can do anything

00:17:17.238 --> 00:17:19.286
and that you don't have
to take no for an answer

00:17:19.286 --> 00:17:20.976
and that you can live your dreams,

00:17:20.976 --> 00:17:24.576
and they were very supportive
of me and so I never really

00:17:24.576 --> 00:17:26.696
questioned whether I could do
anything and then I don't say

00:17:26.696 --> 00:17:29.026
that from a point of arrogance,
but it was a sense of

00:17:29.026 --> 00:17:32.876
if I apply myself if this
is really what I wanna do,

00:17:32.876 --> 00:17:33.709
then I can do it.

00:17:33.709 --> 00:17:37.226
And then, I would spend
so much time studying,

00:17:37.226 --> 00:17:40.236
I wanted to be on the radio
so I would sit this is

00:17:40.236 --> 00:17:44.866
sounds a little pathetic now
but as a 15, 16 year old,

00:17:44.866 --> 00:17:48.739
I sit in my bedroom with
well, a record player,

00:17:49.826 --> 00:17:53.046
a tape recorder and a
microphone and a newspaper

00:17:53.046 --> 00:17:55.169
and I would play radio(clears throat).

00:17:55.169 --> 00:17:58.616
I would talk up a record,
I'd give the weather,

00:17:58.616 --> 00:18:00.676
Sacramento weather it's
gonna be high of 75 today

00:18:00.676 --> 00:18:03.606
at a low of 55, we've got
more coming up but first

00:18:03.606 --> 00:18:06.056
we got a song from whoever it
was and I would do the whole

00:18:06.056 --> 00:18:07.606
DJ thing and then I would go like okay,

00:18:07.606 --> 00:18:09.066
let's take a news break right now

00:18:09.066 --> 00:18:10.616
and I would give the headlines.

00:18:12.106 --> 00:18:15.036
I would this is terrible, I
used to take its unsanitary now,

00:18:15.036 --> 00:18:17.836
but I would take pencils
and pens to put up my mouth

00:18:17.836 --> 00:18:21.199
and work on my diction
and then in high school,

00:18:22.536 --> 00:18:25.056
I got a chance to do the
morning announcements every day,

00:18:25.056 --> 00:18:29.716
I was the guy that would it
was the corner of a lancer,

00:18:29.716 --> 00:18:32.146
so good morning lancers, It's 8.30

00:18:32.146 --> 00:18:34.556
and here's what's happening,
the Spanish Columbus meeting

00:18:34.556 --> 00:18:38.876
in room E2 after school today,
soccer has been canceled,

00:18:38.876 --> 00:18:41.246
whatever I would do that
whole thing and then I would

00:18:41.246 --> 00:18:43.826
'cause I was in this
whole DJ mode I would try

00:18:43.826 --> 00:18:46.719
and do it like a real radio newscast.

00:18:47.705 --> 00:18:50.926
And so it would always end
and that's what's happening

00:18:50.926 --> 00:18:54.104
it's 8.38 and your up to date.

00:18:54.104 --> 00:18:54.937
(chuckles)

00:18:54.937 --> 00:18:58.482
And that was my thing so... (indistinct)

00:18:58.482 --> 00:19:00.375
what a total nerd I was, my goodness.

00:19:00.375 --> 00:19:01.676
(laughs)

00:19:01.676 --> 00:19:04.356
- What did your friends,
did your friends give you?

00:19:04.356 --> 00:19:06.166
Were your friends like
yeah, I know that guy

00:19:06.166 --> 00:19:07.296
or did they give you a hard time?

00:19:07.296 --> 00:19:09.216
'Cause they thought hey,

00:19:09.216 --> 00:19:11.366
this guy is sounding like a real DJ.

00:19:11.366 --> 00:19:12.666
- No, I think people got into it.

00:19:12.666 --> 00:19:14.785
I mean at least they told me they got it

00:19:14.785 --> 00:19:16.016
but I don't know maybe they
were talking behind my back

00:19:16.016 --> 00:19:19.046
but it's a long question
but the idea was man

00:19:19.046 --> 00:19:22.216
I this was what I wanted to do and I knew

00:19:22.216 --> 00:19:25.246
I was gonna have to figure it out and so,

00:19:25.246 --> 00:19:29.416
I got books and I listened
and to radio stations,

00:19:29.416 --> 00:19:32.436
I would, I remember coming
out of Los Angeles big market

00:19:32.436 --> 00:19:34.606
with my tape recorder
and recording all the big

00:19:34.606 --> 00:19:36.636
radio stations there and
then taking them back

00:19:36.636 --> 00:19:38.236
and studying them.

00:19:38.236 --> 00:19:41.479
It was really how I kind
of lived out this fashion.

00:19:42.726 --> 00:19:44.816
- Wow, no I think that's
a huge takeaway for kids

00:19:44.816 --> 00:19:47.236
I mean, anyone any young
person listening is just

00:19:47.236 --> 00:19:49.216
get deep in whatever you're trying to do.

00:19:49.216 --> 00:19:54.046
And I guess(indistinct)
find something else but yeah

00:19:54.046 --> 00:19:56.300
that's I mean that's
a wonderful... people.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:58.549
A lot of people are asking
you about your bass playing

00:19:58.549 --> 00:19:59.920
I mean, do you still play bass?

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:01.781
And I mean, do you literally
like show up at gigs

00:20:01.781 --> 00:20:03.446
and like on bass it's
Lester Holt also anchor

00:20:03.446 --> 00:20:04.336
of the Nightly News?

00:20:04.336 --> 00:20:06.816
- Well, as i said it was another passion.

00:20:06.816 --> 00:20:11.226
And so I wanted to, I was
listening to a band when I...

00:20:11.226 --> 00:20:14.286
and suddenly the bass stood
out to me and I thought

00:20:14.286 --> 00:20:17.216
I love that sound and I
decided I wanted to play bass

00:20:17.216 --> 00:20:20.266
and once again this Mr. Obsessive here,

00:20:20.266 --> 00:20:23.596
I saved up some money
from delivering papers

00:20:23.596 --> 00:20:26.586
and I went to a pawn shop
in downtown Sacramento,

00:20:26.586 --> 00:20:29.956
I bought this cheap base
and a couple of books

00:20:29.956 --> 00:20:32.076
and went on and taught myself the bass

00:20:32.076 --> 00:20:35.146
and started playing in
the junior high jazz band

00:20:35.146 --> 00:20:37.176
and jazz choir and then
I did that in high school

00:20:37.176 --> 00:20:39.866
and a part of college as well.

00:20:39.866 --> 00:20:43.396
So I play, I play electric bass

00:20:44.381 --> 00:20:47.446
and I play the big upright bass as well.

00:20:47.446 --> 00:20:49.603
I did J I had a friend
that used to do gigs

00:20:49.603 --> 00:20:51.936
and here in Manhattan, he'd
asked me to come sometimes

00:20:51.936 --> 00:20:56.226
and join him sit in on a set
and back them up on base,

00:20:56.226 --> 00:20:58.286
and sometimes it was very fun
because they would announce

00:20:58.286 --> 00:21:01.866
the group during the break
and they'd say had on bass

00:21:01.866 --> 00:21:04.231
Lester Holt, you could see
people in the audience go,

00:21:04.231 --> 00:21:06.646
oh my God, that is him not
expecting to see me there.

00:21:06.646 --> 00:21:10.686
And then a couple of years
ago, we formed a band at NPC,

00:21:10.686 --> 00:21:13.586
we call ourselves The Rough
Cuts because most of the members

00:21:13.586 --> 00:21:16.496
are video editors from Dateline NBC,

00:21:16.496 --> 00:21:21.276
but we're all NBC folks and
we would sneak out on Monday

00:21:21.276 --> 00:21:23.656
or Tuesday mornings go to a
studio in Midtown Manhattan

00:21:23.656 --> 00:21:27.666
and rehearse and we would
do gigs here in town from

00:21:27.666 --> 00:21:31.276
time to time, and we're all
kind of in this depression,

00:21:31.276 --> 00:21:33.656
'cause we obviously can't
meet and play right now

00:21:33.656 --> 00:21:38.006
but I always say, so TV news
is my passion right now.

00:21:38.006 --> 00:21:39.986
I always say it's important
to have something else

00:21:39.986 --> 00:21:42.036
in your life though,
because I never wanna be,

00:21:42.036 --> 00:21:45.806
I never want this to define me
that oh he is the anchorman,

00:21:45.806 --> 00:21:49.416
It's what I do, but I'm also the musician

00:21:49.416 --> 00:21:52.136
and there's other facets of my
life so I think it's healthy

00:21:52.136 --> 00:21:54.786
for all of us to discover
something else in our lives

00:21:54.786 --> 00:21:58.196
so we're not pigeonholed and
in some other role necessarily,

00:21:58.196 --> 00:21:59.846
not that I mind being the anchor.

00:22:00.928 --> 00:22:01.761
(chuckles)

00:22:01.761 --> 00:22:04.216
- And what type of music do you all play?

00:22:04.216 --> 00:22:07.389
- Our band plays mostly
pop and rock covers.

00:22:08.256 --> 00:22:11.386
Funny thing is jazz is kinda
my main thing, jazz and pop.

00:22:11.386 --> 00:22:13.496
So, I was never a big rocker.

00:22:13.496 --> 00:22:16.126
So, when I got together
with this group they started

00:22:16.126 --> 00:22:19.186
throwing out these songs and
fortunately I have a really

00:22:19.186 --> 00:22:21.056
good ear and I can pick
things up pretty quickly

00:22:21.056 --> 00:22:23.326
but a lot of them I'd never
heard of, we're playing songs

00:22:23.326 --> 00:22:26.176
I never heard of and I come
home and tell my wife say yeah

00:22:26.176 --> 00:22:30.113
we're doing this song and I
said, it's pretty good she goes

00:22:30.113 --> 00:22:34.945
"yeah, yeah as many discovered
back in 1979 or whatever."

00:22:34.945 --> 00:22:35.778
(laughs)

00:22:35.778 --> 00:22:38.006
So, I would say how we grew
up as kind of helped me

00:22:38.006 --> 00:22:42.469
embrace my inner rocker but
it's been a lot, a lot of fun.

00:22:43.786 --> 00:22:45.186
- No I can only imagine.

00:22:45.186 --> 00:22:47.556
So folks who are regular
viewers know that I used to be

00:22:47.556 --> 00:22:50.607
an abandoned high school but I
suspect I was not as talented

00:22:50.607 --> 00:22:51.719
as you are.

00:22:52.586 --> 00:22:55.996
I was the lead singer and
singing is a very generous term

00:22:55.996 --> 00:22:57.337
for what I did.

00:22:57.337 --> 00:22:59.177
(laughs)

00:22:59.177 --> 00:23:02.826
It yeah it was heavy metal,
now I'm much more calm,

00:23:02.826 --> 00:23:06.796
I'd do more like folksy,
acoustic type stuff.

00:23:06.796 --> 00:23:08.276
One question and there's
a couple of questions

00:23:08.276 --> 00:23:11.146
on this dimension and I'm
curious 'cause it seems

00:23:11.146 --> 00:23:15.346
like the media generally
has evolved over the last

00:23:15.346 --> 00:23:18.156
several decades, there was
kind of the Walter Cronkite

00:23:18.156 --> 00:23:19.509
era back in the day.

00:23:21.426 --> 00:23:25.116
Do you see that it has evolved,
do you see that the media

00:23:25.116 --> 00:23:27.706
is kind of the fourth
estate as kind of holding

00:23:27.706 --> 00:23:30.106
government accountable,
making sure that our democracy

00:23:30.106 --> 00:23:33.616
is informed with the
polarization and sometimes people

00:23:33.616 --> 00:23:35.866
kind of be able to pick
and choose what they get?

00:23:35.866 --> 00:23:38.856
Where do you see where
we are in that narrative

00:23:38.856 --> 00:23:40.176
for the role of media?

00:23:40.176 --> 00:23:42.506
- Well, I think our role
is still the same obviously

00:23:42.506 --> 00:23:46.286
to especially at the
network level to kind of

00:23:46.286 --> 00:23:49.736
be the newscast of record every
night to kind of tell you,

00:23:49.736 --> 00:23:52.126
what's happening in the
world and get you focused.

00:23:52.126 --> 00:23:55.276
At the same time, I recognize
and understand our influence

00:23:55.276 --> 00:23:59.566
isn't what it was when I was
a boy and watching the evening

00:23:59.566 --> 00:24:01.886
programs, people have choices now,

00:24:01.886 --> 00:24:04.436
they have other distractions,

00:24:04.436 --> 00:24:07.096
they have other ways to get their news.

00:24:07.096 --> 00:24:09.776
I recognized by the time
they sit down with us

00:24:09.776 --> 00:24:13.176
at 6.30 eastern time
that they generally know

00:24:13.176 --> 00:24:15.226
the big stories of the day,
they know there's something

00:24:15.226 --> 00:24:19.546
going on with whether the
post office can handle ballots

00:24:19.546 --> 00:24:23.886
and they know some horrible
new Corona numbers came out.

00:24:23.886 --> 00:24:25.413
As a friend of mine told me he says,

00:24:25.413 --> 00:24:27.686
"I want you not to get the news."

00:24:27.686 --> 00:24:30.433
He says, "I want you to
get your take on the news."

00:24:31.416 --> 00:24:33.676
My take being our take is a broadcast,

00:24:33.676 --> 00:24:36.226
the stories that we choose
to cover, how we cover them,

00:24:36.226 --> 00:24:41.226
the perspective we try to
offer and the to help people

00:24:41.346 --> 00:24:43.386
really understand the
importance of these things.

00:24:43.386 --> 00:24:48.386
So on that level I think
we're still vital but again,

00:24:49.256 --> 00:24:52.726
it's not that idea of the
whole world has to kind of stop

00:24:52.726 --> 00:24:53.596
at this time.

00:24:53.596 --> 00:24:57.556
That said many a TV writer
has tried to write off

00:24:57.556 --> 00:24:59.806
the future of the
network evening programs,

00:24:59.806 --> 00:25:02.006
but at the end of the day
there's still 20 million

00:25:02.006 --> 00:25:05.416
or plus people who are watching
every night and I think

00:25:05.416 --> 00:25:08.716
we've all seen big increases
in interest right now during

00:25:08.716 --> 00:25:11.616
this particular trying
time in the country,

00:25:11.616 --> 00:25:14.249
people are craving facts and information.

00:25:15.536 --> 00:25:20.536
We did take I mean, let's
be honest we did take some

00:25:21.246 --> 00:25:25.156
cheap shots from on
high about our business

00:25:25.156 --> 00:25:28.186
and the integrity of journalists
journalism in this country

00:25:28.186 --> 00:25:31.236
but I think we have risen above that.

00:25:31.236 --> 00:25:35.256
I think that people
recognize that they need

00:25:35.256 --> 00:25:38.186
these broadcasts to
really deliver the facts

00:25:38.186 --> 00:25:42.266
and to help understand and
sort out really really noisy

00:25:42.266 --> 00:25:44.576
environment out there of
information that's flying

00:25:44.576 --> 00:25:47.426
at you from all different
directions some true, some not.

00:25:48.756 --> 00:25:51.416
- No, absolutely and I do think
that the kind of the evening

00:25:51.416 --> 00:25:56.416
broadcast news is kind
of the still the go to if

00:25:57.139 --> 00:25:59.926
when you really can't sense
the bias that you can...

00:25:59.926 --> 00:26:03.276
and some other things that
you can get either on cable

00:26:03.276 --> 00:26:08.016
or in the internet generally
just maybe one last question

00:26:08.016 --> 00:26:10.516
we're obviously an
interesting times, you are,

00:26:10.516 --> 00:26:13.186
it's your job to be the
observer and the commentator

00:26:13.186 --> 00:26:15.846
on that for our country, for the world,

00:26:15.846 --> 00:26:18.519
how are you thinking about
the times where and what

00:26:18.519 --> 00:26:19.436
do you think folks are gonna say

00:26:19.436 --> 00:26:22.276
in 10 years about this time?

00:26:22.276 --> 00:26:24.369
- Yeah, I had the conversations
just few days ago I said,

00:26:24.369 --> 00:26:27.196
when the history of this
pandemic is written,

00:26:27.196 --> 00:26:30.726
they're gonna spend a lot
of time talking about how we

00:26:30.726 --> 00:26:33.696
managed to find even
that a way to divide us

00:26:33.696 --> 00:26:36.799
over simple things like
wearing a face covering,

00:26:38.546 --> 00:26:40.486
maintaining the ability to maintain some

00:26:40.486 --> 00:26:42.376
physical distance between people.

00:26:42.376 --> 00:26:44.446
I wouldn't have thought
those would be the,

00:26:44.446 --> 00:26:47.476
those would be issues we'd
be struggling especially

00:26:47.476 --> 00:26:49.396
even with being tainted politically,

00:26:49.396 --> 00:26:52.096
I think that has surprised
me but it'll be part

00:26:52.096 --> 00:26:53.956
of the history of how this has written.

00:26:53.956 --> 00:26:58.386
I think also we'll talk
about misinformation

00:26:59.746 --> 00:27:03.306
and how dangerous it is
to democracy in general

00:27:03.306 --> 00:27:07.006
but certainly on an issue
that is so life and death.

00:27:07.006 --> 00:27:08.986
And I say that with that
hyperbole, I mean it is,

00:27:08.986 --> 00:27:12.116
it's truly a life and death
matter and facts matter

00:27:12.116 --> 00:27:15.066
and words matter and balance matter.

00:27:15.066 --> 00:27:18.986
And, so I think that's gonna
be part of how these times

00:27:18.986 --> 00:27:20.766
will be described.

00:27:20.766 --> 00:27:22.436
I was maybe a little pollyannish,

00:27:22.436 --> 00:27:25.886
I thought when this pandemic broke out,

00:27:25.886 --> 00:27:29.586
I thought this would be one
that we would see the country

00:27:29.586 --> 00:27:32.006
